# Forge: Create Complete Component

## Purpose

This orchestrator rule scaffolds a **complete component** from scratch, following the ideal state defined in `architecture/component.md`.

## Role

You are the Planton Code Partner. This rule orchestrates all forge flow rules to create a production-ready, fully-documented component that matches the ideal state checklist.

## Design Philosophy (NON-NEGOTIABLE): World-Class Per Component

Planton is multi-cloud, but the user is almost always a **single-cloud user** who came to
deploy *one specific thing*. Breadth of coverage is **never** an excuse for a generic or
shallow component. Forge every component — its `spec.proto`, validations, outputs, IaC, docs,
and presets — as if the entire framework existed **solely** to give a world-class
self-service experience for that one resource.

The bar for every component:

> *"If this were the only component Planton shipped, would this be the best self-service
> experience for this resource that exists anywhere?"* If not, it is not done.

- **Derive scope from the source of truth, not from a sibling.** Determine the field set,
  defaults, required/immutable (ForceNew) attributes, and behavior from the provider's
  authoritative reference — read **first**, via local search, not from memory or the web.
  For cloud providers with an official Terraform provider, that clone is the floor
  (e.g. `terraform-provider-aws` `internal/service/<service>/`, and the matching Pulumi SDK).
  For Kubernetes, there is no single Terraform-provider floor: the authoritative reference is
  the pinned upstream artifact for that component (Helm chart `values.yaml`/templates, CRD
  schemas, or core Kubernetes API types), cloned locally for search — never designed from web
  memory or assumptions. Upstream validators/docs are the floor for the field *set*, not
  automatic truth for every constraint; live rejection wins. Design for complete, typed
  coverage of what real advanced use needs — not a generic map passthrough of upstream knobs.
- **Read provider source for FACTS; never copy its code text.** The Terraform provider
  repos are MPL-2.0 (file-level copyleft that must not be mixed into this Apache-2.0
  work), and Terraform core and other HashiCorp repos are BUSL (not open source at all).
  Field sets, defaults, validation bounds, and ForceNew behavior are facts — extract them
  freely; the module implementation is original authorship informed by those facts. If a
  snippet seems so canonical it can only be written one way, that is a fact, not an
  expression — reproduce it from the schema, never paste it. This does NOT restrict normal
  dependency usage: consuming the Pulumi provider SDKs from go.mod and calling their APIs
  is exactly what those Apache-2.0 SDKs are for.
- **Mirror a sibling's STRUCTURE, never its DEPTH.** Copying the nearest sibling's *shape*
  (the four protos, FK refs, output naming, file layout) is correct and required. Copying its
  *field coverage or quality ceiling* is NOT — much of the existing surface was authored
  before this philosophy and is an unreliable proxy for "complete." Matching a shallow sibling
  propagates and compounds shallowness. When a sibling is visibly thin, design to the
  world-class bar instead, and note the gap.
- **Every provider stands on its own merit — never cross-reference providers for design.**
  Users do not choose Planton for multi-cloud breadth; they choose it for a world-class
  self-service experience of the *one* cloud they care about. So a component's design — what
  it models, its fields, decomposition, and depth — is derived only from the target provider's
  own authoritative API, never from how another provider's Planton component modeled a similar
  concept. Every provider is being rebuilt to this same bar concurrently, so a sibling
  *provider*'s component is an outdated, incomplete reference that only drags quality down.
  (Structure is the sole cross-provider constant — see the STRUCTURE-not-DEPTH bullet above.)
- **Docs are part of the experience, not polish.** Dense intent-explaining field comments,
  empathetic CEL messages, richly-commented Terraform and Pulumi module code (explaining
  *why*/trade-offs/provider quirks/non-obvious ordering, not narration -- the module-comment
  bar defined in `architecture/component.md` §4.1), and teaching presets are how a
  coding agent (the primary consumer) and a human learn the component. A bare field set -- or a
  working but uncommented module -- is not world-class even if it is complete.
- **Improve this workflow as you use it.** If forging a component required knowledge this rule
  or its flow rules did not give you — an undocumented mechanism you had to reverse-engineer, a
  wrong or misleading implication, a missing step, or a stale path — amend this rule, the
  relevant flow rule, or `architecture/component.md` in the same session, as
  timeless guidance (never naming a project or a task), so the next agent forging the next
  component never has to re-learn it. The workflow is expected to get sharper with every use.
- **Knowledge lands where its READER lives.** Workflow lessons (above) serve the next
  BUILDING agent — but most lessons, especially live-E2E ones, are CONFIGURATION lessons
  whose reader is the next agent or human writing a manifest, and they must land on the
  surface that reader will actually be looking at: live-caught constraints upstream docs do
  not state go into the spec proto's FIELD comments (the manifest author's surface);
  mechanism explanations go into the module comments of BOTH engines; operational recipes
  and cross-component prerequisites go into `GUIDE.md` / the kind-root `catalog.md` / preset
  explainers. The test: if a future manifest could repeat the mistake, the warning must sit
  on the field its author will be reading when they make it — a lesson recorded only in a
  workflow rule is NOT captured. (The update rule's "Knowledge lands where its READER
  lives" section carries the full reader-to-surface table.)

This philosophy governs every flow rule below; the per-rule steps are how it is executed, not
a substitute for it.

## What Forge Creates

A complete component includes:

✅ **Proto API Definitions** - All 4 proto files with validations and tests
✅ **IaC Modules** - Both Pulumi and Terraform at **100% behavioral parity** (see the Parity Mandate below)
✅ **Documentation** - User-facing README, catalog page, module docs, and the authored GUIDE.md
✅ **Supporting Files** - The e2e test manifest and module docs
✅ **Registry Entry** - Registered in cloud_resource_kind.proto
✅ **Import Recipes** - Provider-catalog entries + component import map, round-trip proven (Phase 6c)
✅ **Presets** - Ready-to-deploy configuration templates (2-3 common patterns)
✅ **Validation** - Build and test validation passed

**Result:** 95-100% completion score when audited against ideal state!

## Parity Mandate (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

A component ships **two** IaC implementations of the SAME contract: a Pulumi
module (`iac/pulumi/module/`) and an OpenTofu/Terraform module (`iac/tf/`). For an
identical `stack-input` they MUST produce the **same** cloud objects, names, labels,
selectors, environment, dependency ordering, and stack outputs. **The default is 100%
behavioral parity.** This is not a "nice to have" -- a silent divergence means the
deployed result depends on which provisioner a resource happens to use, which on a real
platform breaks auth, networking, or data depending on the field that drifted.

**Neither engine is privileged — both must be behaviorally identical.** The arbiter of
"correct" is the proto contract (`spec.proto` + `outputs.proto`) and the intended
behavior, NOT one engine. When the two engines disagree, determine which is right and bring
the other into line; when you author or change one engine, reconcile the other in the SAME
session. (In the forge sequence Pulumi is generated first, so Terraform is reconciled
against the existing Pulumi module — but if the Pulumi module is the one that is wrong, fix
Pulumi.)

**Deviation is allowed ONLY with a strong, documented technical reason.** If the two
engines genuinely cannot match (a provider feature exists in one SDK but not the other, an
operator names a resource differently, etc.), you MUST:

1. Keep the divergence as small as possible (match everything that CAN match).
2. Add an inline comment in BOTH modules at the point of divergence, prefixed
   `# PARITY-EXCEPTION:` (HCL) / `// PARITY-EXCEPTION:` (Go), stating exactly what differs
   and the technical reason.
3. Ensure the divergence does NOT change the stack outputs (the proto contract is the same
   for both engines regardless of internal differences).

An undocumented divergence is a defect, not a deviation. The cross-engine parity gate
(below) and `@audit-planton-component` both treat an unexplained mismatch as a critical gap.

**Reference:** `pkg/iac/MODULE_PARITY.md` is the authoritative parity-dimension checklist
and explains the automated guards (`pkg/outputs` conformance + `planton validate-outputs`).
Read it before authoring either engine.

## Secret-by-default (sensitive fields) (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

A field that holds a secret (a password, token, API/client *secret*, private key/PEM, PSK,
storage/registry access-key *secret*, or a credential-bearing connection string) MUST be
annotated `(dev.planton.shared.options.sensitive) = true` in `spec.proto`. Downstream
platforms then force it to be a managed-secret REFERENCE (`$secret/<slug>`), reject plaintext
at create/update, and resolve the reference just-in-time at deploy -- so the secret never lives
in plaintext in the control plane. The annotation applies to string fields and to the literal
value of `StringValueOrRef` fields, and composes with the foreign-key
`(dev.planton.shared.foreignkey.v1.default_kind)` annotation (a sensitive FK offers a managed
secret OR a cross-resource reference, never plaintext).

A field whose NAME merely *looks* like a secret (a public key, an access-key *id*, a resource
*name*, a key *algorithm*) is NOT a secret -- exempt it with
`(dev.planton.shared.options.sensitive_exempt_reason) = "<why>"`. Never set both options on one
field. Exempt only what the gate actually flags: the guard matches by name PATTERN, and an
exemption on a field the pattern never matches (e.g. `secret_arn` -- `*_arn` names are treated
as references, not secrets) is itself a gate failure ("pointless exemption"). Run
`planton secret-coverage --check` first and annotate from its report; for unflagged
reference-carrying names, let the field comment state the not-secret rationale instead.

This is identified in rule 001 and applied in rule 002. The ratcheting gate
`planton secret-coverage --check` fails the build on any unannotated secret-looking field, so a
forged component is not done until the gate is green. Authority: the option comments in
`shared/options/options.proto`.

Terraform outputs corollary: when a provider object contains ANY sensitive member, never
null-guard the whole object in an output expression (`x.obj != null ? x.obj.leaf : null`) --
comparing a partially-sensitive object yields a sensitive condition that taints the entire
conditional, and `tofu apply` refuses the unmarked output ("Output refers to sensitive
values"). The offline plan gate cannot catch this (output sensitivity is enforced only at
apply). Use direct leaf access with `try(x.obj.leaf, null)` instead: it carries only the
leaf's own sensitivity and degrades to null when the object is absent. Truly sensitive leaf
outputs additionally carry `sensitive = true` as always.

FOREIGN-KEY FIELD-PATH FORMAT (verify against the CONSUMING API)
When wiring `default_kind_field_path` for a network/resource reference, verify which
address format the consuming API accepts -- GCP APIs are inconsistent. Compute-family
APIs accept full `https://` self-links, but several service APIs (AlloyDB,
Service Networking) reject them with "malformed network path" and require the
relative resource path (`projects/{project}/global/networks/{name}`). Pick the
producing kind's output that matches (`network_id` vs `network_self_link`) and record
the expected format in the field comment. A wrong format passes every offline gate and
fails only at live create.

**Run registry-driven guards with a freshly built CLI, never the installed binary.**
`secret-coverage`, `validate-refs`, and every other command that walks the kind registry
reads the protos COMPILED INTO the binary, not the working tree -- a stale installed
`planton` reports false gaps (fields it predates) and false stale-baseline entries
(exemptions it predates), and can equally report false greens. Build from the working
tree first (`go build -o /tmp/planton-dev .` at the repo root -- the root package is the
CLI main) and run the guard through that binary. The same false-result class applies in
reverse: a green from an old binary proves nothing about the current tree. The
`go test ./pkg/secretcoverage/ ./pkg/refcheck/` suites always test the working tree and
are the cheaper equivalent gate when no CLI invocation is needed.

## Usage

```
@forge-planton-component <ComponentName> --provider <provider>
```

**Examples:**
```
@forge-planton-component CloudflareD1Database --provider cloudflare
@forge-planton-component GcpCertManagerCert --provider gcp
@forge-planton-component PostgresKubernetes --provider kubernetes --category workload
```

## Execution Sequence

The following 21 rules execute in order. Each rule is atomic and can be run individually if needed. Rule numbers are stable identities, not execution positions — Phase 2 runs rules 014-015 and Phase 9 runs rule 020 before rule 019.

### Phase 1: Proto API Definitions (Rules 001-006)

1. **001-spec-proto** - Generate spec.proto (minimal schema, no validations)
   - **Includes proactive default identification**: Every field is reviewed against provider docs.
     Fields with well-known defaults (ports, content types, protocols, storage classes, etc.)
     are marked `optional` with `(dev.planton.shared.options.default)` so Planton middleware
     handles defaults centrally. This eliminates defensive default logic in IaC modules.
2. **002-spec-validate** - Add buf.validate rules and CEL validations
   - Also verifies that all identified defaults carry the formal `optional` + default field option.
   - **Also annotates secret-by-default fields** -- every secret-bearing field gets
     `(dev.planton.shared.options.sensitive) = true`; every name-heuristic look-alike that is NOT a
     secret gets `(sensitive_exempt_reason)`; never both. See rule `002-spec-validate`
     ("SENSITIVE FIELD OPTIONS") and the Secret-by-default section below.
   - **Every CEL `message` must be a user-facing, empathetic string** -- see rule `002-spec-validate` ("VALIDATION MESSAGE QUALITY"). These messages are rendered directly in UIs and CLIs. Write them like a knowledgeable colleague, not a compiler.
3. **003-spec-tests** - Generate spec_test.go with validation tests
4. **004-outputs** - Generate outputs.proto (deployment outputs; the message stays `<Kind>StackOutputs`)
5. **005-api** - Generate api.proto (KRM wiring: metadata/spec/status)
6. **006-input** - Generate input.proto (inputs to IaC modules; the message stays `<Kind>StackInput`)

### Phase 2: Registration and Stubs (Rules 014-015)

7. **014-cloud-resource-kind** - Add enum entry to cloud_resource_kind.proto
8. **015-generate-proto-stubs** - Generate .pb.go stubs (make protos)

### Phase 3: Documentation (Rule 007)

9. **007-readme** - Generate the component-root README.md (user-facing GitHub page)

### Phase 4: Supporting Files (Rule 008)

10. **008-e2e-manifest** - Generate the component-root e2e/manifest.yaml (the kind's
    complete, protovalidate-valid example manifest -- embedded as the reference page's
    Example block AND deployed by the E2E framework, which treats its presence as the
    testability marker)

### Phase 5: Pulumi Implementation (Rules 009-011)

11. **009-pulumi-module** - Generate module/ (main.go, locals.go, outputs.go, resources), richly commented to the module-comment bar
12. **010-pulumi-entrypoint** - Generate entrypoint (main.go, Pulumi.yaml -- nothing else)
13. **011-pulumi-readme** - Generate iac/pulumi/README.md

**CR-rendering kinds — verify the generated SDK can actually CARRY the CRD
before adopting it.** crd2pulumi flattens a CRD field typed as a map of
NESTED objects (schemas using `additionalProperties` with object values —
e.g. name-keyed backup-storage maps) into `map[string]map[string]string`,
a generated type that structurally cannot hold the nested bodies; any
adapter that smuggles heterogeneous values through it panics at runtime
inside the provider's input marshaling. When the CRD carries such a field,
render the WHOLE CR with `apiextensions.NewCustomResource` +
`kubernetes.UntypedArgs` (a plain nested `map[string]interface{}` — the
exact twin of the Terraform module's `yamlencode` locals), record the
reason in the builder's header comment, and do not generate the typed
package at all: an SDK no module consumes is dead weight. The operator's
server-side schema validation plus the live E2E lanes are the shape-error
safety net the typed SDK would have been.

**Operator kinds — own every cluster-scoped object the operator creates
at RUNTIME.** Some operators register cluster-scoped singletons at startup
(the classic case: a fixed-name Fail-closed `ValidatingWebhookConfiguration`
pointing at a Service in the operator's own namespace). Kubernetes never
garbage-collects a cluster-scoped dependent of a namespaced owner, and an
operator that finds the object already present typically refreshes only
parts of it — so left to the operator, UNINSTALL strands an object that
can brick the whole cluster's admissions. Read the operator's startup
source in the clone for such registrations; when found, the module renders
the object itself (created BEFORE the release, deleted with the resource),
ignores the operator-managed fields (CA bundles, ownerReferences) via
`ignore_changes` / `IgnoreChanges`, and — on the Pulumi side — annotates it
`pulumi.com/patchForce: "true"` so server-side apply adopts an instance a
previous operator created instead of failing on the field conflict. The
E2E verifier asserts the object exists while deployed and is GONE after
destroy.

### Phase 6: Terraform Implementation (Rules 012-013)

14. **012-terraform-module** - Generate tf/ (variables.tf, main.tf, locals.tf, outputs.tf, provider.tf), richly commented to the module-comment bar
    - **variables.tf is GENERATED, never hand-mirrored**: run
      `planton tofu generate-variables <Kind> --output-file catalog/<provider>/<kind>/iac/tf/variables.tf`
      from the working tree (after the proto stubs regenerate) and commit the generator's
      output (`tofu fmt` it). A hand-written mirror silently drops fields — the module then
      never renders them and every offline gate stays green (the drift surfaces only live,
      as a spec field that does nothing). Re-run the generator after every spec change.
    - Rule 012 ends with the mandatory **PARITY WITH PULUMI** reconciliation (see that rule).
      Terraform is authored AFTER Pulumi in the sequence, so it is reconciled field-by-field
      against the existing Pulumi module — fixing whichever engine is wrong, since neither is
      privileged (the proto contract + intended behavior decide correctness).
15. **013-terraform-readme** - Generate iac/tf/README.md

### Phase 6b: Cross-Engine Parity Gate (orchestrator gate -- no flow-rule file)

After BOTH engines exist, the orchestrator runs an explicit reconciliation gate before
moving on. This is a hard gate: do not proceed to presets/validation while an
**undocumented** divergence remains. Walk the parity-dimension checklist from
`pkg/iac/MODULE_PARITY.md` and confirm Pulumi (`iac/pulumi/module/*.go`) and Terraform
(`iac/tf/*.tf`) agree on each, for an identical `stack-input`:

- **Namespace / scope source** -- both read the same spec field (e.g. `spec.namespace`),
  not a derived or resource-id value.
- **Resource naming basis** -- created objects (CRs, operator names, secret names, pod
  annotations) key off the SAME field on both sides (`metadata.name` is the established basis).
- **Labels** -- identical keys AND values (the `kuberneteslabelkeys` set; kind value is the
  `CloudResourceKind` enum string; conditional labels like `id` appear under the same condition).
- **Selectors** -- pod/service selectors match the labels the operator/chart actually applies
  (a wrong selector "succeeds" but matches zero pods).
- **Spec feature coverage** -- every field in `spec.proto` is honored by BOTH engines
  (backup, restore, ingress, env injection, sizing, etc.). A field implemented on one side
  and ignored on the other is a parity defect.
- **Dependency ordering** -- the same `depends_on`/relationship edges exist on both sides.
- **Stack outputs** -- both engines emit output shapes that flatten onto the SAME
  `outputs.proto` fields. Verify with the automated guard, not by eye:
  ```bash
  planton validate-outputs --kind <Kind> --module-dir catalog/<provider>/<kind>/iac/tf --sample-outputs <sample>.json
  ```
  Expect full proto population and zero unmapped outputs. Add/extend the per-kind case in
  `pkg/outputs/conformance_test.go` so the parity bar is enforced in CI thereafter.

For any dimension that genuinely cannot match, apply the Parity Mandate's
`PARITY-EXCEPTION:` protocol (documented in BOTH modules) -- otherwise fix the lagging
engine in this session. Report the gate result (parity achieved, or the explicit list of
documented exceptions) in the progress output.

### Phase 6c: Import recipes (orchestrator step -- no flow-rule file)

Every component with a Terraform module ships its import-ID recipes, so the
platform's guided import can derive identifiers instead of asking the user
(`pkg/iac/importmap/README.md` is the authoritative reference):

1. **Provider catalog** (`catalog/<provider>/aa_import/catalog.yaml`):
   an entry per resource type the module declares -- the import-ID format from
   the PROVIDER's import documentation (read the provider docs/source, never
   guess), the Pulumi type token, and any `config_only_attributes` (attributes
   that exist only in IaC configuration -- `force_destroy`-class delete
   behavior, and the kubernetes provider's `wait_for_*` lifecycle knobs
   (`wait_for_rollout`, `wait_for_load_balancer`,
   `wait_for_service_account_token`) -- import can never carry them; declare
   them when the resource type first enters the catalog, or every consumer's
   round-trip lane fails on the same knob later). When a provider's importer
   fails to read back ONE nested block (found only by running the round-trip),
   declare it as a dotted `write_normalized_attributes` sub-path
   (e.g. `"spec.update_strategy"`) -- never tolerate a whole structural
   attribute like `spec`, which would blind the proof.
2. **Component map** (the component-root `iac/import-map.yaml`): the value source per
   `{placeholder}` -- prefer derivable sources (metadata.name, spec field,
   stack output, ARN part, address key); `where_to_find` is mandatory when
   nothing derives. The file's presence IS enrollment: the offline conformance
   guard (`TestImportMapConformance`, rides `make test`) auto-discovers it, and
   the platform bundles it into the import wizard's catalog data.
3. **Live round-trip proof** (merge gate, non-negotiable): run the component's
   E2E lane with `PLANTON_E2E_IMPORT_ROUNDTRIP=1` (terraform engine). The
   phase deploys the fixture, sets its state aside, re-imports every resource
   blind through the recipes, and requires the follow-up plan to propose no
   real change. A new or changed import map does not merge without this lane
   green; record the result in the README ledger
   (`pkg/iac/importmap/README.md`).

Design the e2e scenario so every module resource actually deploys (a
conditional satellite that no scenario exercises is a recipe that has never
been proven). When a resource cannot be composed live, say so in the profile
header and treat its recipe as offline-validated only.

### Phase 6d: Provider-parity manifest (orchestrator step -- no flow-rule file)

For providers instrumented with committed schema artifacts
(`pkg/providerparity/schemas/`), author the component's
`iac/provider-parity.yaml` NOW, while the modeling judgment is fresh --
retrofitting it later means re-deriving every naming decision:

1. Run the accounting for the new kind from the repository root:
   ```bash
   planton provider-parity --provider <provider> --ga-schema <ga-schema> --kind <Kind>
   ```
2. For every unaccounted argument, record the judgment in the manifest:
   a `mappings` entry (rename or subtree; e.g. plural repeated field vs the
   provider's singular block), an `exclusions` entry with the REASON, or --
   for a plumbing resource whose arguments are module decisions (API
   enablement, module-wired parent linkage) -- the resource-level `internal`
   disposition. For every uncovered spec field, either fix the mapping or
   record a `specExclusions` entry (platform-level fields).
3. Re-run with `--check`; the kind must be at total accounting and OUT of
   `pkg/providerparity/baseline.yaml` (a new kind never ships pre-baselined
   debt). File presence IS enrollment -- the `lint.provider-parity` CI lane
   holds the kind to the manifest from this commit on.
4. The new kind changes the accounting, so regenerate the committed public
   parity page in the same change: `make generate-provider-parity-report`
   (its drift test fails CI whenever the accounting moved and the page did
   not). Also record the kind's breadth effect: if the dispositions ledger
   (`pkg/providerparity/dispositions/`) carried the consumed resources as
   planned or deferred, the accounting now flags those entries stale --
   remove them.

For a provider without schema artifacts, skip silently -- the gate reports
NOT INSTRUMENTED there.

### Phase 7: Presets (Rule 018)

16. **018-presets** - Generate initial presets (2-3 common configuration templates)
    - Reads `spec.proto` to identify field combinations that produce distinct deployment patterns
    - Creates ranked YAML manifests + companion markdown in the component-root `presets/`
    - Follows conventions from `architecture/presets.md`

### Phase 7b: Catalog data sidecars (orchestrator step -- no flow-rule file)

Every component ships three authored data files; the anatomy gate requires
them and the `lint.catalog-data` lane holds their content to schema:

1. **`cost.yaml`** (component root, schema `finops/componentcostprofile/v1`):
   declare the billing model, the always-on baseline charges, and the spec
   fields that drive the bill (field paths are spec-relative proto names --
   the gate resolves each against the served contract). State the estimate
   exclusions honestly: what no manifest can predict (traffic, storage
   growth, data transfer).
2. **`controls.yaml`** (component root, schema
   `compliance/componentcontrolprofile/v1`): examine EVERY control in
   `catalog/_compliance/controls-catalog.yaml` -- enforced_by_default /
   configurable (with the field path) / not_applicable (with the reason).
   Every enforced/configurable claim carries evidence a reviewer can verify
   (provider guarantee, spec default, spec validation rule, or module
   source). Never name compliance frameworks here; the central crosswalks
   own that mapping.
3. **`iac/permissions.yaml`** (schema `iac/componentpermissions/v1`): the
   least-privilege permissions the runner's cloud principal needs, grouped
   the way a policy author would group them. Derive from the module's
   declared resources and mark every entry `derived` (notes cite the
   terraform resource types it covers); the live capture lane upgrades
   entries to `proven`. Defend every wildcard in notes.

`catalog/aws/awsalb` and `catalog/aws/awsdynamodb` are the reference
examples. Validate with `go test ./pkg/finops/... ./pkg/compliance/...
./pkg/iac/permissions/...` -- file presence is enrollment, so the sidecars
are gated from this commit on.

### Phase 8: Final Validation (Rules 016-017)

17. **016-build-validation** - Verify component Go code compiles AND the Pulumi entrypoint
    builds as the release builds it. Runs BOTH `go build ./catalog/<provider>/<kind>/...`
    (recursive, catches module/proto errors) and
    `go build -o /dev/null ./catalog/<provider>/<kind>/iac/pulumi` (non-recursive,
    the release contract -- requires a `package main` at the entrypoint root). Also asserts the
    entrypoint lives at `iac/pulumi/` root with no `main/` or `entrypoint/` subdir, AND that
    `iac/pulumi/Pulumi.yaml` exists: without the project file `pulumi stack init` fails at
    deploy time with a misleading "pass the fully qualified name" error, and NO offline check
    (go build, tofu validate, spec tests) can catch its absence -- only a live run does.
18. **017-test-validation** - Run `go test -v ./catalog/<provider>/<kind>/v1alpha1/` (verify component tests pass)
    - Validates spec_test.go tests all buf.validate rules
    - Ensures validation rules are syntactically and semantically correct

### Phase 9: Catalog Page + Guide + Reference Regeneration (Rules 020, 019)

19. **020-catalog-md** - Write the component's `catalog.md` (THE catalog page, at the
    component root) following `_rules/docs/write-planton-component-catalog-md.mdc`:
    the head-shape machine contract (the bundle parses the H1 and first sentence as
    the kind's display name and description), the mandatory section structure, the
    three Deploy arms, judgment-first Key Configuration, and patterns naming the
    presets from rule 018. Runs FIRST in this phase — the guide grounds its claims
    partly on the catalog page. The anatomy gate requires this file at the component
    root.
20. **019-guide** - Seed the component's `GUIDE.md` (the authored wisdom layer at the
    component root) from what this forging session learned -- internal conventions,
    judgment calls, operational recipes, parity accounting, diagram impact -- following
    `_rules/docs/write-planton-component-guide.mdc`.
    Then run `make generate-reference`: the new kind's reference page, its guide head
    link, and the catalog indexes all materialize in one pass, and the reference
    freshness gate fails any new kind whose pages were not regenerated.

### Phase 10: Logo (Rule 021)

21. **021-logo** - Author the component's `logo.svg` under the logo law: one kind, one
    glyph. The provider's official mark, unaltered, ONLY when the kind IS the product;
    the Planton brand mark on Planton's own kinds; a Planton-drawn glyph that says what
    the kind is everywhere else (every child of a product, every configuration object,
    every grant), never containing or modifying a provider mark. Every file carries its
    provenance in a `<desc>`. Then `go test ./pkg/cataloglogo/` -- three rules
    (`malformed-svg`, `missing-provenance`, `shared-glyph`), each failure saying what to
    do; a new kind never enters the gate's baseline.

## Error Handling

- Each rule retries up to 3 times on fixable errors
- Build/compilation errors are fixed automatically when possible
- If a rule fails after 3 attempts, the process stops with clear error message
- You can resume from the failed rule after fixing issues

## Progress Tracking

The orchestrator provides progress updates:
