## Post-Forge Validation

After forge completes, validate the component:

```
@audit-planton-component <ComponentName>
```

Expected result: **95-100% completion score**

If score is lower, the audit report will identify gaps and suggest fixes.

## Post-Forge Live E2E Validation (provider credentials)

Steps 018–019 prove the component compiles and its schema validates. **Live E2E**
proves the IaC modules actually create, verify, and destroy real resources via
the provider API. Every component ships an `e2e/profile.yaml` + `e2e/scenarios/*.yaml`,
and the provider harness (`catalog/<provider>/aa_e2e/`) runs them
on both Pulumi and Terraform.

Live E2E needs provider credentials. **Never ask the human to paste them, and never
fabricate them.** Acquire them with this do-first, two-tier protocol. (Note: `gh`
can `secret list` names but CANNOT read Actions secret VALUES — they are write-only.
Only the CI runner can read them.)

**Profile honesty is non-negotiable.** `e2e/profile.yaml` may claim
`status: green` with `validated_provisioners` entries ONLY after a live
deploy → verify → destroy run has actually passed on those provisioners.
Until then, author the profile as `status: pending_proof` with an empty
`validated_provisioners` list — the status that says "fully authored and
offline-validated, live lanes not yet run." Reserve `status: deferred` (with
a `deferred_reason` stating what blocks it — the field is `deferred_reason`;
protojson silently drops unknown keys, so a misspelled key vanishes without
an error) for kinds with a KNOWN blocker or failure. A profile that
overclaims is a lie in the release ledger; flip it to green in the same
session the lanes pass, never before.

**The authoring and proving phases may be executed by different sessions.**
Everything up to and including the offline gates (specs, both engine modules,
spec tests, presets, docs, import maps, scenarios/fixtures/verifiers compiled
via `e2e-build`/`e2e-vet`, static proofs) is the authoring phase and ends
with the profile at `pending_proof`. The live-E2E phase below is the proving
phase: whichever session executes it owns the kinds completely while proving
— including spec/preset/docs changes when live evidence demands them — and
finishes by flipping the profile, filling `validated_provisioners`, landing
live-caught lessons on the surfaces their readers use, and regenerating the
CI matrix. Note the mechanics either way: the test entrypoints SKIP
components whose profile is `pending_proof` or `deferred`, so such a profile
can never run its own lanes — flip the profile to green immediately before
executing the lanes, and if they do not pass by session end, flip it back in
the same commit. Honesty is judged on the COMMITTED state, not the
mid-session working tree.

### Wire the kind into the provider E2E first (required for either tier)

A new kind is only exercised once it is registered in the provider harness:

- `aa_e2e/verify/verifier.go` — add a verifier keyed on the component dir name.
  SaaS providers use the `apiPathVerifier` pattern (`&apiPathVerifier{component:
  "<component>", pathFormat: "<api-path>/%s"}`); cloud-SDK providers (e.g. AWS)
  implement the three-method `Verifier` interface against the provider SDK,
  keyed on the API's own identifier for the resource and treating the provider's
  typed not-found error as the absent signal.
- `e2e/<provider>/<provider>_test.go` — add `Test<Kind>_Pulumi` / `Test<Kind>_Terraform` entrypoints. Scenarios are auto-discovered; the entry funcs are not.
- **Test-name discovery is registry-driven.** The GitHub Actions matrix builder in
  `pkg/e2e/profile/discover.go` maps component directory names (e.g. `awslambda`) to
  Go test `-run` regexes via `crkreflect.KindFromString` +
  `ExtractKindNameByKind` — **do not** add hand-maintained PascalCase entries per
  kind. The only exceptions live in the small `testNameOverrides` map, reserved for
  genuine, verified deviations where the test entrypoint spelling differs from the
  enum name; a new kind whose entrypoints follow the enum name needs no entry.

**Composed scenarios are registry-driven, with a scenario-level extension for
optional composition.** The harness resolves a component's E2E dependencies
from the kind registry's `kind_meta.prerequisites` annotation (transitively),
NOT from the presence of an `e2e/prerequisite.yaml` file — that file is merely
the install manifest used once a dependency is declared (falling back to the
dependency's `scenarios/minimal.yaml`). Declare a registry prerequisite only
when the dependency is genuinely required for the kind to function (a required
ref) — the registry is the honest statement of what a kind needs to deploy,
and it propagates transitively into every consumer's dependency chain, so a
padded registry taxes every downstream lane. For OPTIONAL references that only
a particular scenario composes (an instance's subnet, a cluster's capacity
group), the scenario manifest declares its own additional dependencies via the
`planton.dev/e2e-prerequisites` metadata annotation (comma-separated). Each
entry is either a kind name (e.g. `"AwsSubnet,AwsSecurityGroup"`) or a
repo-relative manifest path (recognized by containing a `/`). Declared kinds
expand through their own registry graphs, merge and dedupe with the
component's registry prerequisites, and deploy in topological order — so
annotating the top of a chain is enough. Manifest-path entries deploy that
exact document as an EXTRA INSTANCE of whatever kind it declares (how a
scenario gets a SECOND instance of a kind, e.g. a peering's remote network),
after the kind-driven chain, each preceded by its own not-yet-deployed
transitive prerequisites — a path entry never substitutes for the kind's
install profile. Unknown kinds and self-references fail loudly. Singular AND
repeated `StringValueOrRef` fields both resolve, refs nested inside (repeated)
spec messages resolve too, and a repeated list can mix literals with
references. Audit the annotation against EVERY `valueFrom` kind the scenario
references: a reference to a kind that is neither a registry prerequisite nor
annotated stays UNRESOLVED, and the failure mode is engine-asymmetric and
live-only — the Terraform lane fails at apply with "Null value found in list"
(the tfvars renderer emits a null element for the unresolved ref) while the
Pulumi module silently sends an empty string, which some AWS APIs accept, so a
green Pulumi lane can mask the gap until the Terraform lane runs.

**Install manifests declare their own composed fixtures the same way.** The
resolver also reads the `planton.dev/e2e-prerequisites` annotation on each
prerequisite's OWN install manifest (recursively, cycle-checked) and orders
those fixtures BEFORE the declaring kind — so when a dependency's install
manifest references sibling fixtures via `valueFrom` (the zip-backed Lambda
scenario referencing the S3 object-set fixture that stages its code), the
chain deploys in resolvable order with no per-consumer wiring. Put the
annotation on the install manifest that carries the references; consumers of
that kind inherit the ordering automatically.

**But a MANIFEST-PATH fixture's own file annotations are NOT edges.** The
recursive annotation walk above applies to KIND install profiles
(`prerequisite.yaml` / minimal scenarios); a path entry contributes only its
kind's registry prerequisites. When one path fixture depends on another (a
ServiceAccount fixture needing a Namespace fixture), list BOTH paths on the
scenario's annotation in deploy order — path entries deploy exactly in
listed order. An annotation placed on the path fixture's own file is
silently ignored, and the failure is live-only (the dependent fixture
deploys into a namespace that does not exist).

**Multi-instance prerequisites.** A prerequisite install profile
(`e2e/prerequisite.yaml`) may carry multiple `---`-separated documents of the
kind when real topologies need more than one instance — e.g. a load balancer
or EKS cluster requires two subnets in different availability zones, so the
subnet profile publishes a two-AZ pair. Each document deploys as its own
stack, and its outputs are captured under its own `metadata.name`. Resolution
is name-aware: a `valueFrom` reference picks the instance whose name it
addresses; when the name matches no deployed instance but exactly one
instance of the kind exists, that sole instance is used (so scenario names
are not coupled to profile names); a name matching none of SEVERAL instances
fails loudly. A reference's explicit `kind`/`fieldPath` win over the field's
`default_kind`/`default_kind_field_path` annotations.

**A prerequisite kind needs its own verifier and install manifest.** The
harness VERIFIES every dependency it deploys (and its absence after
teardown), so declaring a kind in `prerequisites` requires that kind to
have a registered verifier in `aa_e2e/verify/verifier.go` and an install
manifest (`e2e/prerequisite.yaml` or `scenarios/minimal.yaml`) -- even if
the kind has never had E2E coverage of its own. A dependency without a
verifier fails every consumer's DEPENDENCIES-UP phase with "no verifier
registered", after the resource has already deployed.

**Consumer-specific fixture shapes live in the shared profile.** There is
exactly ONE prerequisite profile per kind, shared by every consumer that
declares the kind in `prerequisites` — so when consumers need differently
SHAPED instances (e.g. IAM roles with different trust policies and managed
policies: an instance-profile role, an EKS control-plane role, an EKS worker
role), the profile publishes one named document per shape and each consumer's
scenario references its instance by name. Fixtures must carry everything the
consumer requires of them (a role fixture attaches its own managed policies),
because modules never mutate a resource they merely reference — see the
composition principle in the doctrine. Extra fixture instances deployed for
other consumers are harmless when the resource is free and fast (IAM roles);
weigh the cost for expensive kinds.

**Run live E2E serially per account — never parallelize lanes.** Prerequisite
fixtures deliberately use FIXED manifest names and CIDRs (stable identities are
what make orphan sweeps and name-aware resolution possible), so two concurrent
runs in the same account collide on IAM role names and subnet CIDRs and both
fail their DEPENDENCIES-UP phase. Batch a family's runs one after another; the
time saved by parallelism is an illusion here.

The Kubernetes analog: kind-cluster lanes for DISJOINT components may run in
parallel, but lanes must serialize whenever one lane's component is another
lane's prerequisite fixture, or the component is a cluster singleton (a fixed
release name / cluster-scoped APIService or CRDs) — the two runs contend for
one identity.

**Kubernetes cluster profiles: scenarios that need a cluster the default kind
cluster cannot be.** Some components change what the CLUSTER ITSELF is — a CNI
must install onto a cluster created WITHOUT a default CNI, and behavioral
proofs that depend on such a component (NetworkPolicy enforcement needs an
enforcing CNI) inherit the requirement. The harness supports this via the
`planton.dev/e2e-cluster-profile` scenario annotation: the test entrypoints
route the annotated scenario to a dedicated persistent kind cluster built for
that profile, created lazily on first use (see the aa_e2e README for the
profile table and mechanics). Rules when designing lanes around a profile:
the profile cluster is typically single-node, so chart defaults sized for HA
(a 2-replica anti-affine operator) can never fully schedule — scenario
manifests must size those knobs to the topology or the atomic install waits
forever. The same class hides in RESOURCE REQUESTS, not just topology: read
every companion workload's default requests at the pin — including computed
factors (memcached charts request memory at 1.2× the allocated size, so an
"8192MB cache" default renders a ~9.8Gi request that no small node can ever
schedule; the pod stays Pending and the atomic install rolls the whole
release back, reading as "the component won't install"). A default request
that exceeds a small node's allocatable is a product caveat, not just a lane
problem: teach it on the sizing field's spec comment and in the dev preset,
and size it in every kind-lane scenario (the disk_size class). A CNI-less
cluster's nodes are NotReady BY DESIGN until the CNI
component installs, so never gate the profile's creation on node readiness —
assert the NotReady→Ready transition in the component's install verifier
instead, where it becomes part of the proof; and behavioral scenarios that
merely CONSUME the profile component declare it a scenario-level prerequisite
(annotation), never a registry prerequisite — the component under test
deploys fine without it on ordinary clusters.

**Real-cluster lanes fit what is already on the cluster.** A real cluster
(`aws-eks`, `gcp-gke` profiles) arrives with residents — the GKE management
cluster carries cert-manager and CloudNativePG from the Planton operator; the
EKS batch carried its own load-balancer controller — and the singleton kinds
forbid a second copy. Two scenario annotations declare the fit, both per
scenario: `planton.dev/e2e-prerequisite-install-manifest: "Kind=path"`
SUBSTITUTES the kind's install profile (the manifest takes the kind's slot in
the chain — how the Postgres GKE lane installs the operator kind in its
plugin-only posture beside the resident CloudNativePG, the very shape under
test), and `planton.dev/e2e-resident-prerequisites: "Kind"` prunes a kind the
cluster already has (refused on scenarios not pinned to a real-cluster
profile — nothing is resident on a harness-owned cluster). A manifest-path
entry in `e2e-prerequisites` can never do either: it only ever ADDS an
instance. Real clusters also add TIME: an instance whose anti-affinity makes
the autoscaler add a node pays node provisioning plus a cold image pull
(live-measured ~4 minutes per instance on GKE) — size verifier readiness
windows for the topology on fresh nodes, not one pod on a warm kind node.

**Restore proofs read the backup the seed wrote — through `${E2E_SETUP:NAME}`.**
A backup's storage location exists only after the seed takes it, and it is
exactly what the restore manifest must declare. The setup script publishes
`NAME=value` lines to `$E2E_SETUP_OUTPUT`; the runner expands the scenario's
`${E2E_SETUP:NAME}` tokens after SETUP, before VALIDATE. Never hardcode a
backup name only one run produced, and never let two lanes share an
archive path: carry `${E2E_RUN_ID}` in the backup destination — Barman
refuses to archive into a path holding another cluster's WAL (the cluster
stays healthy while ContinuousArchiving stays false forever; live-caught),
and PBM keys its metadata by the storage path. The seeded evidence must be
readable by the credential the proof authenticates with: a marker table the
seed created as the superuser fails the application user's read with
"permission denied" on a credential that authenticated perfectly (live-caught
— own the table by the application role).

**Behavioral proofs whose driver CR is served by the component under test are
VERIFIER-OWNED.** Scenario fixtures deploy BEFORE the component under test, so
a fixture cannot carry a custom resource whose CRD the component itself
installs (a KEDA ScaledObject, an operator's workload CR) — and chaining a
second instance of the component to fix the ordering trips its
fixed-release-name singleton. The honest shape: fixtures carry only what needs
nothing from the component (a plain target Deployment); the behavioral
verifier applies the driver CR via kubectl AFTER the install assertions pass,
asserts the behavior, and deletes what it applied in a defer — active cluster
interaction is established verifier grain (`kubectl top` gates, live curl
probes), and verifier-owned cleanup keeps a failed assertion from leaking a CR
that blocks later lanes. And never kill a lane mid-install to "free" the cluster: an
interrupted Helm install orphans its release in `pending-install` state, which
blocks every later install of the same name with a misleading name-in-use
error. Sweep with `helm uninstall <release> -n <namespace>` (plus the fixture
namespaces) before re-running.

**Verifier assertions against OTLP/protojson APIs match BASE64 IDs, not
hex.** An API that returns protojson-encoded protobuf (Tempo's trace query,
any OTLP surface) renders `bytes` fields — trace and span IDs above all — as
BASE64. A verifier that greps the response for the hex ID it pushed will
hold the successful response and still report failure, and every retry
budget then expires "unable to retrieve" what was retrieved on the first
try (caught live). Convert the pushed ID's bytes to base64 for the success
check; keep hex only for human-facing log lines.

**Verifier proof objects are deleted CONFIRMED, never fire-and-forget —
controllers add finalizers to objects they observe.** A `--wait=false`
delete in a cleanup defer looks clean and leaks: sidecar services
(archival watchers, result recorders) attach their own finalizers to
workload CRs, and a proof object still pending such a finalizer when the
destroy phase kills its processor can never finish deleting — it wedges
its namespace in Terminating and poisons every later lane on the shared
cluster (caught live: a proof run's archival finalizer held a namespace
for half an hour). Cleanup = issue the delete, POLL the object gone
within a bounded budget while the stack that serves the finalizer is
still alive, and strip finalizers as the last resort — a verifier-owned
proof object's durability guarantees are meaningless on its way out.

**A pod-replacement proof gates on a NEW pod UID, never on phase or
readiness alone.** A terminating pod keeps reporting phase Running (and
can remain Ready) for its whole grace period, and a StatefulSet's status
lags the deletion — so any wait keyed on phase/readiness straight after
`kubectl delete pod --wait=false` returns instantly against the DYING
pod, and the "post-replacement" assertions then run against the old
server (caught live twice; for a bootstrap-gated server the follow-up
bootstrap call is silently burned as a no-op on the old pod and the real
replacement never gets it). Capture `metadata.uid` BEFORE the delete and
wait for the same-named pod with a DIFFERENT uid via the shared
`waitForPodReplaced` helper in `aa_e2e/verify/` — `requireReady=true`
for self-readying servers, `requireReady=false` (phase Running) for
pods whose readiness is deliberately gated on out-of-band bootstrap the
verifier performs next.

**A web UI's identity is proven through its own API, never brand text in
the SPA document.** A modern UI's index.html is an opaque asset bundle —
its text content (even the product name) is an upstream build detail that
changes without notice (caught live: a UI whose document carries no
product-identifying text at all). Read the UI server's source at the pin
for an unconditional API route (a settings/config endpoint) and assert
its response CONTRACT (a known JSON key), not its wording.

**An auth-gate probe targets a route the SERVER SOURCE proves requires
authentication — anonymous-by-design endpoints are never gates.** Products
with a public/private visibility model (registries, artifact stores, wikis)
deliberately serve anonymous reads of the public subset, so "any endpoint
without credentials answers 401" is a false contract (caught live: an
unauthenticated project listing answered 200 with the public subset against
a correctly-secured install). Pick the gate route by reading the handler at
the pin (an explicit require-authenticated guard), and when the product's
real guard is per-resource visibility, prove THAT surface too: create the
proof resource private and assert the anonymous data-plane access is
rejected — the strongest gate is the one a customer's security actually
depends on.

**A browser-session gate discriminates on the server's OWN login
contract, never on a redirect-followed page fetch.** Web apps redirect
anonymous page requests to their login page, so "GET a protected page
with a redirect-following client and expect non-200" is a false
contract: the refused session lands on the login page and reads 200
exactly like a signed-in one (caught live: the probe reported
wrong-password sign-ins as successes against a correctly-secured
install, on every lane). Read the login handler at the pin for the
refusal status (e.g. a 403 form re-render vs a success redirect) and
assert THAT on the login POST itself; then prove the session on an
authenticated API identity route (200 + the identity for a signed-in
jar, 401/403 for an anonymous one) — API routes answer status codes,
never redirects, so they discriminate under any client.

**A verifier's tunnel Service names and pod selectors are read from the
chart's Service and pod templates at the pin, never inferred from the
Deployment-name pattern.** A chart that names its Deployments
`<fullname>-<component>` may still name the component's Service plain
`<fullname>` (caught by review: a tunnel to `svc/<name>-coordinator`
against a chart whose coordinator Service is `<name>` — every lane would
have failed at tunnel open). Read `service-*.yaml` for the name and the
pod template's `labels:` block for the selector, and verify the chosen
selector is UNIQUE against every sibling workload the chart renders
(workers, schedulers, hook Jobs).

**A template read is only as truthful as the clone's checked-out REF —
verify it against the pinned chart version before trusting the read.**
When one clone serves two pins (the app tag for image/server truths, the
chart tag for template truths), its working tree sits on whichever ref
was needed last, and a template read from the wrong ref produces
confident false conclusions (caught live: a pod-selector "fix" built on
an app-tag checkout whose in-tree chart was several minor versions
behind the served chart — the old chart stamped legacy `app`/`release`
labels, the served chart stamps `app.kubernetes.io/*`, and the "fixed"
selector matched zero pods). Before any template-derived claim, confirm
`git describe --tags` (or the in-tree `Chart.yaml` version) matches the
pinned CHART version — and when live pods disagree with a template read,
trust the pods and re-check the ref.

**A chart's CRD posture is read from WHERE the CRDs live, never assumed
from chart ownership language.** `crds/` directory = Helm's install-once
posture: created on first install, never upgraded, NEVER removed on
uninstall — destroy must assert the CRDs SURVIVE (caught live: a destroy
assertion built on "release-owned CRDs delete with the release" failed
against a chart whose CRDs ride `crds/` and are untouched by uninstall).
Templated CRDs (in `templates/`) are release-owned and delete/keep per
their annotations; hook-applied CRDs follow the hook's own posture. The
posture claim in the spec/docs/verifier must cite which of the three the
chart actually uses at the pin.

**Scenarios needing owner-arranged external credentials declare them with
the `planton.dev/e2e-required-env` annotation.** Unset `${E2E_ENV:...}`
tokens fail expansion LOUDLY by design (right when a batch bootstrap was
supposed to export them, wrong for credentials only a human can arrange —
a sandbox account PAT, an external tenant). The annotation lists the
variables (comma-separated); lanes then SKIP the scenario with the reason
wherever they are absent — an honest, self-documenting deferral that
keeps CI green — and run it live wherever the owner exported them. Record
the matching deferred-E2E row with the unblock.

**One-way-hash credentials in E2E artifacts must be VERIFIED pairings.** When
a scenario or fixture carries a one-way hash (a bcrypt htpasswd tenant
password, a pre-hashed basic-auth entry) whose PLAINTEXT a verifier must use
live, the pairing is unprovable by reading and silently breaks: the hash
validates as a manifest, every offline gate passes, and only the live 401
exposes a hash that never matched its claimed plaintext. At authoring time,
generate AND mechanically verify the pair (`htpasswd -nbBC 10 <user> <pass>`
then `htpasswd -vbB <file> <user> <pass>`), record both commands in the
artifact's comment, and keep the plaintext as a named constant beside the
verifier that uses it — the two sides change only together.

**Isolate the run from OTHER suites on the same machine.** Serial-within-your-
provider is not enough when a different provider's E2E suite runs concurrently
on the same machine (multiple agents share these workstations): the suites
share the global Pulumi state (`~/.pulumi` login/credentials) and `$TMPDIR`
(where each run's ephemeral file backend lives), and the interference surfaces
as `no stack named '<stack>' found` on a stack that was just deployed — the
resources are then orphaned because teardown cannot find the state either.
Machine-level temp cleaners can destroy the ephemeral backend the same way.
Run every live lane with a private, durable environment:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/planton-e2e-tmp/pulumi-home
export TMPDIR=~/planton-e2e-tmp PULUMI_HOME=~/planton-e2e-tmp/pulumi-home
go test -tags e2e ./e2e/<provider>/... -run 'Test<Kind>' -v -count=1
```

(`-count=1` matters too: a cached `go test` result silently replays the old
run instead of touching the cloud.) **Export `GITHUB_TOKEN` for every live
lane** (`GITHUB_TOKEN=$(gh auth token)`): a fresh private `PULUMI_HOME` has no
plugin cache, so every Pulumi command resolves plugins through anonymous
GitHub API calls, and a few back-to-back lanes exhaust the anonymous rate
limit. The failure then lands WHEREVER the next plugin resolution happens —
including `pulumi destroy` and `pulumi stack rm` during DEPENDENCIES-DOWN —
so fixtures orphan and every later lane fails its DEPENDENCIES-UP with
`EntityAlreadyExists`/`BucketAlreadyOwnedByYou` name conflicts. The log line
`GitHub rate limit exceeded ... You can set GITHUB_TOKEN` buried in a
teardown error is the signature; the token lifts the limit entirely. **The
private TMPDIR must be a SHORT path** — never a nested `mktemp -d` chain. Terraform/OpenTofu providers are
go-plugin processes that hand-shake over unix domain sockets created under
`$TMPDIR`, and macOS caps a socket path at 104 bytes; a deep TMPDIR (e.g.
`/var/folders/.../T/tmp.X/tmp.Y/tmp.Z`) overflows that limit and every
Terraform lane fails at provider-schema time with the cryptic
`Unrecognized remote plugin message` — which looks like a corrupted plugin
but is purely the socket path length. Pulumi lanes are unaffected, so a
"Pulumi passes / Terraform fails to even instantiate the provider" split is
the signature of this failure. If a lane still fails this way, the
leaked resources remain recoverable through the backend directory left under
`$TMPDIR` — `pulumi destroy --stack <name>` with `PULUMI_BACKEND_URL=file://…`
from the dependency's module directory, then sweep the account.

**Prerequisite teardown failures FAIL the run.** The harness destroys
prerequisites in reverse order after the component's own lifecycle, and any
destroy/stack-removal failure (e.g. the ephemeral backend state vanished
before teardown) fails the run's DEPENDENCIES-DOWN phase — a green run
therefore guarantees zero orphaned prerequisites. When that phase is red,
real cloud resources may still exist: sweep the account for the scenario's
`planton-e2e`-tagged resources before re-running.

**A service's FIRST-ever use in an account can fail on its service-linked
role.** AWS services that operate through a service-linked role (SLR) —
Redshift's `AWSServiceRoleForRedshift` is one — auto-create that role on the
account's first create call, and the very first call can fail with
`InvalidParameterValue: Unable to assume the SLR on the customer account`
while the role propagates. The SLR then exists (verify with `aws iam
get-role --role-name <SLR>`), and the retry succeeds. Treat this error class
as a first-use transient, not a module bug: confirm the SLR now exists and
re-run the lane before touching any code.

**Reference field paths must be output-backed.** The E2E resolver (like the
production flattening it mirrors) resolves `valueFrom` references against the
referenced instance's captured STACK OUTPUTS — a `field_path` under
`status.outputs.*` resolves; one under `metadata.*` cannot and fails the
composed scenario. Design rule, not just a test rule: point every
`default_kind_field_path` at a stack output. If a kind's natural join key is
its name, export a `name` output and reference that — never `metadata.name`.

### Tier 1 — Local run (preferred: fast on-machine loop)

Local credentials live in a gitignored `.env.e2e` at the repo root (ignored via
`.gitignore`; NEVER commit it). The harness reads credentials from environment
variables — discover the exact names by reading the harness `Setup`
(`aa_e2e/harness.go`); e.g. Auth0 uses `AUTH0_DOMAIN`, `AUTH0_CLIENT_ID`,
`AUTH0_CLIENT_SECRET`.

```bash
# Confirm which provider secrets exist (names only) and match the harness env vars:
gh secret list

# If .env.e2e exists, source it and run ONLY this component's scenarios:
set -a; source .env.e2e; set +a
go test -tags e2e ./e2e/<provider>/... -run 'Test<Kind>' -v -count=1
```

If `.env.e2e` is absent, do NOT invent it: tell the human exactly which variables it
must contain (from the harness) and that it is gitignored, then use Tier 2 so the
validation still happens this session.

### Tier 2 — CI dispatch (fallback: Actions injects the secrets)

```bash
git push -u origin <feature-branch>
gh workflow run e2e-<provider>.yaml --ref <feature-branch>
RUN_ID=$(gh run list --workflow=e2e-<provider>.yaml --branch <feature-branch> --limit 1 --json databaseId -q '.[0].databaseId')
gh run watch "$RUN_ID" --exit-status --interval 20
```

Then confirm the new kind actually ran (not skipped) and passed both provisioners:

```bash
gh run view "$RUN_ID" --log | rg -i '<kind>'
# expect: ✓ <Kind> pulumi <scenario> ... ✓ <Kind> terraform <scenario>
```

Keep self-contained scenarios self-contained: a `minimal.yaml` should not depend on
resources that may not exist in the test tenant (e.g. an Auth0 role's `minimal`
scenario omits permissions so it needs no pre-existing resource server). Add a
richer scenario (seeding the dependency) when you need live coverage of dependent
fields.

**An upstream doc's example class is not evidence it ships in the
image.** Docs and in-repo examples routinely reference plugins/classes
that the SHIPPED image no longer carries (distributions trim default
classpaths; examples go stale against their own images). Before
designing a proof, preset, or documented example on "class X runs on
the stock image", verify against the RUNNING image itself — the
service's own plugin/capability listing (e.g. a Connect worker's
connector-plugins endpoint) or the image's directory contents. Every
offline gate passes on the stale claim; only a live lane exposes it,
as a resource that sits NotReady with "class not found". When the live
check contradicts the upstream example, the live truth is what goes on
the reader surfaces (spec comments, presets, docs), marked as verified
live.

**The same discipline applies to the official image's VARIANT when the
module owns the manifests.** Projects that publish a bare package image
(`pip install <app>` on a slim base) often publish a dependency-complete
sibling (`-full`, `-all`, an extras matrix) — and the bare variant may
be unable to BOOT the arms the spec models (database drivers,
object-store clients, even the auth feature's own dependency can live
in extras). Before pinning a default image, read the image's OWN build
file at the pin (its Dockerfile/extras list, not the tag name) and
verify every modeled arm's dependency is in the chosen variant; probe
the chosen tag's registry manifest. A driverless default crash-loops
at first boot behind every green offline gate. Grep the server's own
startup/create-app path for hard-required environment (signing keys,
secret-key material a framework refuses to start without): each such
requirement is module-generated Secret material wired by design, never
discovered by a customer at boot — and when the server requires the
value be consistent across replicas, sourcing every replica from ONE
module-owned Secret satisfies it by construction.

**Model the upstream's built-in sandbox arm when one exists.** Cloud-talking
addons (DNS publishers, secret-store operators, ...) cannot prove their
provider hop on a kind cluster — but many upstreams ship a first-class
in-process backend for exactly this (a DNS provider that holds records in
memory, a secret store that serves declared literals, a node-autoscaler
simulation provider). Model it as a typed spec arm, documented as
sandbox/test-only: users get a safe evaluation arm, and the kind-cluster
E2E lanes get a FULLY verifiable path (the controller runs its real
reconcile loop and the verifier can assert end-state, e.g. a synced
Secret's contents), instead of settling for install-only assertions.
Also read the controller's error handling before designing credential-less
lanes: some providers crash-loop on a bad credential (a 4xx at first sync)
while others log-and-retry — only the latter class supports an
install-proves-out lane with no real account. **Before authoring a fixture
for a sandbox arm, check whether the CHART renders the sandbox's
supporting objects itself** (grep the templates for the arm's
`cloudProvider`/mode conditional): pre-creating an object the chart also
templates fails the install with Helm's release-ownership error ("exists
and cannot be imported into the current release"), and the failure is
live-only — every offline gate passes. When the chart is self-contained
for the arm, the scenario needs no fixture at all.

**Keep-on-uninstall CRDs come in TWO classes with different blast radii —
know which one the chart ships before writing the guidance.** (1)
Chart-TEMPLATED CRDs kept via `helm.sh/resource-policy: keep` are
release-owned: they retain `meta.helm.sh/release-namespace` ownership
annotations, so a later install of the same release in a DIFFERENT
namespace hits the release-ownership error on objects that "should not
exist" — live-only, and easy to trigger by moving a scenario between
namespaces; scenarios for such kinds need a stable, dedicated namespace.
(2) `crds/`-directory CRDs are created RAW by Helm on first install with
NO ownership metadata at all (verified in the Helm source) — they pin
nothing and collide with nothing, but Helm also never UPGRADES them:
a chart-version upgrade runs new controller code against old CRDs. That
is why such charts land on the DERIVE branch of the decision tree below
(the module re-applies the pinned version's CRDs on every bump) rather
than on a spec/docs story of "apply the new CRDs yourself". One more
corollary of surviving CRDs, either class: operators that AUTO-DETECT an
integration by CRD presence (cert-manager is the classic) are fooled by
controller-less remnants on a long-lived cluster — a dead-webhook dry-run
failure at reconcile, not install. Two release-ownership corollaries:
(1) never place a component's E2E scenario in `kube-system` or another
shared system namespace — the cleanup phase asserts namespace absence,
which a system namespace can never satisfy (and upstream docs defaulting to
kube-system are a docs default, not an E2E-compatible one); (2) when
sweeping kept CRDs manually, enumerate by the `meta.helm.sh/release-name`
annotation, never by an API-group name grep — CRD bundles routinely span
groups (Karpenter's fleet CRDs live in karpenter.sh, karpenter.k8s.aws AND
autoscaling.x-k8s.io), and one missed CRD reproduces the collision.

**Every chart that carries CRDs is placed on ONE decision tree before
any module code is written — the catalog has exactly three shapes and no
fourth.** Read the chart at the PIN (`helm pull --untar`; `crds/`
directory, `templates/`, and the values file) and choose:

1. **The chart owns its CRD lifecycle** (CRDs templated behind an
   install switch AND a keep switch that stamps
   `helm.sh/resource-policy: keep` — the cert-manager shape). The spec
   carries the shared `crds { install, keep_on_uninstall }` message
   (both `optional bool`, both defaulting true) and the module maps
   those two fields into the chart's own values. Nothing else: no
   module-side CRD resources, no second copy of the schema.
2. **Upstream publishes a SEPARATE CRD chart** (Karpenter's
   `karpenter-crd`). The module installs it as a second, kept
   `helm_release` ahead of the operator release, pinned to the same
   version, and the operator release skips CRDs.
3. **Everything else — the module DERIVES the CRD set from the pinned
   version.** This branch covers charts that template CRDs release-owned
   with no keep knob (an uninstall would cascade-delete every CR and its
   data), charts that ship CRDs only in Helm's install-once `crds/`
   directory, and operators whose CRDs are published only as a bundle
   beside the chart. The module renders the pinned chart with its CRD
   switch ON and CRDs included (`data "helm_template"` under the helm
   provider 3.x; the Helm SDK's client-only dry-run install with CRDs
   included on Pulumi — both return the same document set, verified),
   or fetches the pinned upstream bundle by a URL derived from the
   version; filters the documents to `kind: CustomResourceDefinition`;
   applies each one keyed by its OWN `metadata.name` as a KEPT resource
   (server-side apply with force; `apply_only` on `kubectl_manifest`;
   the classic-yaml ConfigGroup with a retainOnDelete transformation
   and the shared upsert provider seam on Pulumi) BEFORE the release;
   and installs the release with `skip_crds = true` and the chart's own
   CRD switch pinned OFF after the `helm_values` merge (a user override
   must never re-arm the cascade). The mechanics are shared, never
   hand-rolled per kind: one Go package for Pulumi
   (`pkg/iac/pulumi/pulumimodule/provider/kubernetes/keptcrds`, over the
   engine-neutral `pkg/kubernetes/helmcrds`), one canonical Terraform
   block (`helm_crds.tf`, written by `planton tofu generate-helm-crds`)
   that every module carries byte-identically under a drift test.
   Per-kind code supplies one contract, the twin of the other engine's:
   `keptcrds.Args` on Pulumi and the `local.helm_crds_args` object on
   Terraform — the chart identity, the release's own values, the render
   override that turns the chart's CRDs on, the API versions the chart
   gates on, the bundle URL when one exists, and the kind's policy.

**Which rendered CRDs the module owns follows ONE rule, stated in the
primitive and repeated nowhere else.** Helm has two CRD surfaces: the
`crds/` directory (install-once; `skip_crds` governs it) and the
templates (ordinary release resources). A module that supplies a render
override has turned the chart's switch on for the render and pinned it
off for the release, so EVERY CRD the render produces is the module's — a
typed kind's case. A module that supplies no override renders exactly
what the release renders: the `crds/` surface is the module's, and CRDs
the chart templates stay Helm's. Templated CRDs the chart marks
`helm.sh/resource-policy: keep` are the chart owning its lifecycle (branch
1) and need nothing; templated CRDs WITHOUT that mark would be deleted
with the release along with every custom resource built on them, so the
plan refuses them with their names and the remedies unless the spec
accepts Helm-managed CRDs. Only the generic Helm kind, whose chart is the
user's, supplies no override and carries that acceptance dial
(`crds.allow_helm_managed`); a typed kind always pins the switch. A
post-render hook that would strip or keep-mark templated CRDs generically
is not available where the engines run (the Pulumi SDK's post-render
takes a bare command and the runner ships no helper binary), so the
refusal, not a hook, is the design. A typed kind sets `expect_crds` so a
render that yields no module-owned CRD is a failure (the chart's switch
was renamed, or the chart stopped shipping CRDs); the generic kind does
not, because most charts carry none.

**Two invariants, both machine-enforced.** (1) **A module is its
directory.** Nothing outside `iac/tf/` or `iac/pulumi/` is ever read:
release packaging zips exactly that directory, and the Pulumi binary
lane runs from a generated workspace that holds only `Pulumi.yaml` — a
relative read of a sibling path returns nothing at best and plans zero
resources at worst, behind a green e2e lane that copied a tree releases
never ship. The anatomy gate forbids `iac/crds/`; the self-containment
guard (`hack/guards/ensure_modules_are_self_contained.sh`) forbids
parent-path reads in either engine. (2) **A module derives, never
copies, what a pinned artifact already carries.** A vendored CRD is
frozen at one version while `chart_version` moves with the user, so the
schema silently stops matching the operator it serves — the API server
accepts fields the controller ignores, or rejects fields the controller
needs. There is no fail-loud count guard that fixes this; only
derivation does.

**Facts the derive branch rests on, all verified at the pin.** The
render runs with the REAL release name and namespace, so release-derived
values inside a CRD (a conversion-webhook `clientConfig`, a cert-manager
`inject-ca-from` annotation) come out correct without token substitution
— and a runtime reconciler (cert-manager's CA injector) is the only
correct keeper of a conversion webhook's trust, which is why such a kind
models cert-manager as a REQUIRED posture. Charts gate templates on
`.Capabilities`: pass the API versions the chart checks
(`api_versions`/`APIVersions`) or the render silently drops them.
Templated CRDs arrive in the rendered manifests; `crds/`-directory CRDs
arrive on the separate CRDs surface — read BOTH and filter by kind.
Rendering needs the chart repository at PLAN time; the install needs it
anyway, so nothing new is required of the network, but the failure now
happens earlier and must say so. The render consults the local Helm
repository configuration: a stale repository entry on the operator's
machine fails every render and every install identically. Per-version
bundle URLs must be the form that survives the next release (Apache's
`dist` tree holds only the CURRENT release; `archive.apache.org` holds
every release). Retained CRDs are RE-ADOPTED on the next install: the
Pulumi provider's create fails AlreadyExists even under SSA unless the
retained resources route through the upsert seam
(`pulumikubernetesprovider.GetWithKubernetesProviderConfigUpsert`) —
ordinary resources keep create-conflict semantics; `kubectl_manifest`
server-side apply adopts natively.

**A FIRST-PARTY chart is derived from the PUBLISHED package at the pin,
exactly like any other chart — and the typed spec may only claim LIVE
proof for what that published schema carries.** The working tree's CRD
routinely runs AHEAD of the last published chart (unreleased schema
changes between cuts); deriving from the published artifact makes the
staged-from-tree mistake structurally impossible, but the discipline on
the spec remains: diff the published CRD's field set against the spec,
and keep any spec field the published schema would prune OUT of the live
scenarios (offline hack manifests carry it) until a published line
admits it.

**Every failure explains itself — in the module, the guard, and the
verifier.** A precondition, an error, a plan-time refusal, or a status
text for the CRD lifecycle carries three parts in order: what was
OBSERVED (with the value — the chart, the version, the CRD name, the
owner found), what it most likely MEANS (one root cause per message,
never a list of maybes), and the exact NEXT STEP (a field to change, a
command to run, an upstream fact to check). Text that names only the
mechanism ("count mismatch", "conflict", "not found", "precondition
failed") is a defect. Wording is stable so an agent can match it and
act without a human. The anticipated set, each with a negative e2e lane
asserting on the three parts: chart repository unreachable at plan time;
pinned version not published (HTTP and OCI); CRD already exists owned by
another release or tool; CRD schema downgrade attempted; render produced
zero CRDs for a chart expected to carry them; the chart templates CRDs
as Helm-managed resources without the keep mark and the spec has not
accepted that; CRD apply denied by RBAC; bundle URL 404; stale local
repository configuration; a private repository refusing the credentials;
no cluster connection at all (a local deploy with neither KUBECONFIG nor
the default kubeconfig — refused before any engine starts, never left to
the providers' "connection refused to localhost"); a required CLI flag
left empty (refused with the flag's copyable example, never a bare
"please provide"). The CLI carries the same three labels
(`ui.Failure` in `internal/cli/ui`; `pkg/failure` is the shared type)
so a person or an agent reads one vocabulary in the module, the guard,
and the command line. A change that adds a branch to the shared
mechanics adds that branch's failures and lanes in the same change.

**Where each explanation lives is layered, not either-or.** A module
refuses IN PLACE wherever its own code can see the fact (a precondition
or postcondition on a data source's result; a check in the Pulumi
program), so an ejected module run with plain `tofu` still explains
itself as far as HCL allows. A failure that kills the read itself (a
repository host that does not resolve, an API server answering
Forbidden at apply) reaches HCL only as the provider's raw text, and the
layer that runs the engine — the CLI's engine runners, the e2e harness,
the platform runner — recognizes those texts (`pkg/failure.Explain`)
and appends the same three parts the Pulumi twin gives in-process, from
one vocabulary, never repeating what the module already said. Two facts
the CRD primitive verified the hard way: server-side apply with force
adopts a kept CRD on reinstall AND silently takes over a CRD nobody
stamped, so every CRD about to be written is read BY NAME first and one
read answers both "is it ours" (the source label) and "is it newer" (the
version stamp) — a CRD someone else owns is refused with that owner
named (Helm's release annotations, another module's stamp, the field
managers, or the honest absence of any mark) and two remedies (leave it
with its owner through `crds.install = false`, or hand it over with the
two printed `kubectl` commands); and a permission probe
(SelfSubjectAccessReview) or a read runs only when the render produced
something to write, so a chart with no CRDs is never refused for a CRD
right it does not need.

**An operator's DESTROY contract is read from its controller source,
never assumed from a sibling kind's posture.** Operators split into two
teardown classes: finalizer-processed (the controller holds the CR's
deletion open and unwinds components itself — deletion HANGS without a
running operator) and owner-reference garbage collection (children carry
ownerReferences; Kubernetes GC completes teardown with or without the
operator). The classes invert the destroy-ordering story, the delete
timeout's meaning, the absence verifier's design (one-shot vs polled
drain), and whether cluster-scoped satellites strand (a namespaced owner
NEVER garbage-collects a cluster-scoped dependent — those need the
operator's own janitor or an honest residue note). Grep the controller
for `Finalizer`/`DeletionTimestamp` before writing any destroy prose,
verifier, or ordering guidance; a sibling kind's posture is evidence
about that sibling only (caught in review: destroy guidance authored on
a finalizer-based sibling's contract against a GC-based operator — every
claim inverted).

**Two chart-audit probes that only fail LIVE — run them at the pin,
before any design lands.** (1) HEAD every DEFAULT image reference's
registry manifest: chart defaults can point at registries that no longer
exist (verified live — an entire upstream registry namespace deleted),
and the failure is a pod stuck in ImagePullBackOff behind a green
offline gate. A dead default gets re-pointed at the maintainer's live
repository at the same tag, loudly commented and overridable. (2)
Compute the chart's LONGEST child-name suffix: charts truncate their
fullname at 63 characters but not the names derived from it, so an
ordinary release name can push a child Service past the Kubernetes name
cap and fail mid-install; when the budget breaks realistic names, pin
`fullnameOverride` to metadata.name and record the resulting name-length
budget on the spec.

**Map the chart's image-value SHAPE, not the spec's.** Charts take the
image reference either as one combined `image.repository` or SPLIT
across `image.registry` + `image.repository` with the registry
defaulting to docker.io — read the pod template at the pin to know
which. When the spec's air-gap field carries a full reference
("my.registry.com/org/app") and the chart wants the split shape, the
module must split it (the first path segment is a registry exactly when
it contains a dot, a port colon, or is `localhost` — the container
runtimes' own rule); mapped verbatim, the chart re-prefixes its default
registry and every mirror override dies in ImagePullBackOff. This class
is identically wrong in BOTH engines, so cross-engine parity review
never catches it — only the chart template read does.

**A scalar-looking chart value may be a STRUCTURED block — the templates'
`.Values` accesses are the shape truth, not values.yaml alone.** A value
that reads like a plain setting (an instance identifier, a toggle-with-
options) is sometimes a nested object whose sub-fields the templates
access directly (`.Values.x.enabled`, `.Values.x.explicitID`); rendering
a bare string onto it makes Helm's template evaluation fail at install
("can't evaluate field ... in type string") on any manifest that sets
the field — identically in BOTH engines, invisible to every offline
gate that never renders the chart. Before mapping any spec scalar onto
a chart value, grep the templates for that value's field accesses; a
single `.Values.<key>.<subfield>` hit means the module renders the
structured shape, however plain the spec field stays.

**Read every chart default that PLACES resources by namespace or
cluster-scoped name — silence adopts it.** Charts ship list/map defaults
whose entries name namespaces (or fixed cluster-scoped names) and plant
real resources there — a runner-RBAC namespace list defaulting to
`["default"]` creates ServiceAccounts and RoleBindings in the cluster's
`default` namespace on every install that does not override it. A module
that renders such a list only when the spec declares entries has
silently adopted the chart's placement; destroy assertions then fail
zero-orphan checks in namespaces the component never claimed. Either
ALWAYS render the list (the spec's entries, else an explicit empty list
— lists REPLACE under Helm merge semantics) or adopt the default
consciously with the reason taught on the spec field.

**An operator distribution may auto-create its own workload CR — find
the switch and disable it, or the declaration kind fights its manager.**
Some operator release manifests ship a config default (an env var / a
defaults ConfigMap key) that makes the operator CREATE its own instance
of the very CR a companion declaration kind renders — a
default-configuration singleton with a fixed name, materialized at
controller startup. Left on, the operator and the declaration module
co-write one object and fight through server-side apply field
ownership; installs are order-dependent and updates flap. When splitting
an operator kind from its workload-declaration kind, sweep the
distribution for auto-install/auto-create switches (grep the controller
source for the env keys its startup reads) and patch them OFF in the
module as a design invariant, not a spec knob — then have the E2E
verifier assert the CR does NOT exist after installing the operator
alone (the regression is silent otherwise).

**Fenced env tokens must satisfy the field's own CEL pre-expansion —
embed the token inside the validated shape.** `${E2E_ENV:PLANTON_E2E_*}`
tokens expand at lane runtime, but `validate-manifest` and the scenario
gates parse the RAW file: a token standing alone in a field whose CEL
demands a format (an `https://` URL prefix, a scheme, a prefix-anchored
pattern) fails validation before any lane runs. Put the constant part
of the format in the manifest and the token inside it
(`https://github.com/${E2E_ENV:PLANTON_E2E_GITHUB_REPO_PATH}`) so the
committed manifest passes its own CEL and the env var carries only the
variable segment. Fields with no format CEL (credentials, ARNs, names)
take the bare token.

**Behavioral drivers against PERSISTENT external stores need run-unique
names.** A verifier-owned driver CR whose name doubles as a key in an
external store (a Velero Backup's name keys its object-store records) must
carry a run-unique suffix: on a real, persistent bucket the store copy
outlives the CR — deleting the CR never purges the store and the location
sync even resurrects it — so a second lane reusing a fixed name fails with
"already exists in object storage". Ephemeral in-cluster fixtures (a MinIO
that dies with the scenario) mask this class completely: the kind lanes
stay green while every real-cluster lane after the first fails.

**Verifier-owned driver resources keyed in PERSISTENT external stores need
run-unique names.** A verifier-owned CR whose name doubles as a key in an
external store that outlives the lane (a Velero Backup's records in the
object-store bucket, which the BSL sync even resurrects after the CR is
deleted) can never reuse a fixed name across lanes: the second lane fails
with an "already exists" that no kind-cluster run reproduces (ephemeral
fixtures start empty; the real-cluster batch's bucket persists). Derive the
name per run and let the resource's own TTL expire the store residue.

**Saturation fixtures must not await rollout.** A pressure fixture DESIGNED
to leave trailing replicas Pending (capacity saturation for preemption or
autoscaling proofs) deadlocks any deploy that awaits workload readiness —
set the fixture's skip-await knob and say in the manifest why it is
load-bearing, or the fixture times out its own deploy on both engines.

**Expanded/resolved manifest copies must keep the scenario's basename.**
The runner rewrites scenario manifests to temp copies at two points (run-ID/
environment token expansion and foreign-key reference resolution), and
verifier dispatch keys behavioral variants off the scenario NAME in the
manifest path. A randomly named copy silently demotes the scenario to its
plain verifier — the lane still passes, install-grade, and the behavioral
claim becomes an overclaim nobody notices. Both rewrite sites preserve the
basename by contract; any future manifest-rewriting step must do the same,
and a behavioral lane's proof lines (the verifier's own `[verify]`
behavioral output) — not its PASS alone — are what a session may cite as
evidence.

**Soft-delete-reserved identifiers need the `${E2E_RUN_ID}` token.** Some cloud
resources reserve their user-chosen identifier long after deletion (e.g. GCP
workload identity pools/providers and KMS key rings hold their IDs through a
soft-delete retention window, with no undelete-on-create). A scenario or
prerequisite manifest that hardcodes such an identifier can never be deployed
twice — the second engine's run minutes later collides with the first run's
soft-deleted ghost. Embed `${E2E_RUN_ID}` in the cloud-side identifier field
(e.g. `poolId: e2e-smoke-${E2E_RUN_ID}`); the shared runner expands it to an
engine-scoped per-run value in both scenario and prerequisite manifests. Keep
`metadata.name` fixed — prerequisite FK resolution and human debugging key off
it, and only the cloud-side identifier needs uniqueness. Note the raw manifest
will not pass `planton validate` until the token is substituted; validate it
with a sample value substituted.

When the identifier's character class forbids hyphens (letters/numbers/
underscores only, e.g. a Vertex AI `deployed_index_id`), use the
`${E2E_RUN_ID_UNDERSCORE}` variant — the plain token carries the runner's
hyphenated engine suffix and would break the field. The hold class extends
beyond names: a failed/undeploying Vertex AI DeployedIndex holds its
user-chosen sub-resource ID with a 400 that SURVIVES deleting the parent
endpoint, so a fixed ID fails across engines and back-to-back runs even
with fresh prerequisites (see `e2e/README.md`).

**Regional outputs: emit the spec region, not the provider attribute.** On
released google/google-beta 6.x lines the `.region` attribute on regional
resources (NEGs, subnetworks, regional health checks) may be a region self-link
(`https://www.googleapis.com/compute/v1/projects/.../regions/us-central1`) rather
than the plain name (`us-central1`). Stack outputs and E2E verifiers expect the
plain name — export `var.spec.region` / `pulumi.String(spec.Region)` instead of
the resource's `.region` attribute when the output proto documents a region name.

